{#-
  TranslateGemma's own prompt, transcribed from `chat_template.jinja` lines 612-622 of the
  pinned snapshot (google/translategemma-12b-it, revision d1b225e1caa1...).

  WHY THIS IS RENDERED HERE INSTEAD OF BY THE SERVER. TranslateGemma's chat template expects
  structured content -- `{type, source_lang_code, target_lang_code, text}` -- and vLLM strips
  those custom keys before the template sees them, so `/v1/chat/completions` returns HTTP 400
  (verified against the live service; vLLM does not support custom content fields). The client
  therefore renders the prompt and posts it to `/v1/completions`. That makes this file the whole
  interface to the model, which is exactly why it belongs in the prompt store with a SHA256
  rather than inline in a client: a changed prompt changes model behaviour, and the hash is what
  ties an English artifact to the prompt that produced it.

  DO NOT PARAPHRASE THIS. It was paraphrased once, from memory, and the omitted sentence was
  "Produce only the {target} translation, without any additional explanations or commentary."
  Measured on the real service without it: a 22-word unit came back as 197 tokens beginning
  "Here's a translation that aims for accuracy and nuance:", and "Mmm." came back as "Okay, I
  understand. I will do my best to provide accurate and nuanced English translations of any
  Spanish text you provide." With the sentence restored: 19 tokens of clean translation, and
  "Hmm.". Verify any edit against the snapshot file, never against memory.

  `bos_token` from the template's line 585 is deliberately absent: vLLM's /v1/completions
  prepends BOS itself (`add_special_tokens` defaults true), so including it would double.
-#}
